    "markdown": "**Top of mind:** OpenAI publicly slowed development of its upcoming Astra model after it crossed the company's \"critical\" cybersecurity capability threshold — the first time a frontier lab has throttled a flagship on cyber-risk grounds. That turns safety evaluation from research overhead into release-gating infrastructure: cyber red-teaming, dangerous-capability evals, and secure test environments are now what unblocks launches. Otherwise a quiet weekend; Francis deSouza formally takes the Scale AI CEO seat tomorrow.\n\n## Competitor moves\n\n- Nothing notable today — weekend lull across the data vendors. The live thread remains fallout from the Aug 5 Forbes China exposé (Surge, Mercor, Turing, AfterQuery supplying Chinese labs); no lab or Washington response has surfaced yet. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/)\n\n## New projects & opportunities\n\n- **Cyber-capability evaluation is becoming release-gating procurement.** OpenAI's disclosure that Astra hit its Preparedness Framework's critical cyber threshold — and that development was slowed while safeguards are hardened — means every lab now needs pre-threshold early-warning systems: expert-authored offensive-security tasks, CTF-style test environments, and human red-team trajectories. A concrete new line item to pitch, and cyber/security talent is a pool Indian vendors can staff quickly. [OpenAI](https://openai.com/index/responding-next-frontier-critical-cyber-capabilities/), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/07/openai-says-it-slowed-astra-model-development-over-security-concerns/)\n\n## Frontier lab movements\n\n- **OpenAI slowed work on Astra over security concerns (Aug 7).** An internal review found the unreleased model made significant advances in agentic coding and cybersecurity, crossing the threshold at which it could independently identify and execute attacks on well-protected systems; fallout coverage continued through Aug 8. Expect rivals to face the same bar — and to buy the evals that prove where their models stand. [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/07/openai-says-it-slowed-astra-model-development-over-security-concerns/), [OpenAI](https://openai.com/index/responding-next-frontier-critical-cyber-capabilities/)\n- **Anthropic confirmed an in-house chip-design team (Aug 5), targeting ~50% lower per-token inference cost.** The hardware–software co-design effort is anchored by Clive Chan (ex-OpenAI chip team, ex-Tesla Dojo), with Samsung reported as manufacturing partner, alongside its existing Nvidia/AMD/TPU/Trainium stack. Cheaper inference frees budget for the post-training data and evaluation spend where models actually differentiate — and signals Anthropic is planning for sustained training scale. [Quartz](https://qz.com/anthropic-custom-ai-chip-design-team-claude-080526), [Tom's Hardware](https://www.tomshardware.com/tech-industry/anthropic-to-build-its-own-co-designed-custom-ai-accelerator-for-inferencing-workloads-samsung-reported-to-be-partnering-with-the-claude-ai-maker-for-manufacturing), [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/jonmarkman/2026/08/06/anthropic-enters-the-ai-chip-race-with-in-house-chip-team/)\n\n## Research papers\n\n- **Adaptive allocation of human-evaluation budgets (Zouhar et al., posted to arXiv early August).** Vilém Zouhar, Julia Kreutzer, Alon Lavie, Tom Kocmi, Matt Post, Ondřej Bojar, and Mrinmaya Sachan formalize multi-model human evaluation as best-arm identification in a multi-armed bandit: sampling adaptively on interim rankings concentrates annotator budget on the most competitive models, making evals faster and cheaper at the same discriminative power. Worth folding into eval delivery now — adaptive sampling protects margins as clients squeeze eval budgets, and it's a methodology differentiator in proposals. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03437)\n\n## Leadership movements\n\n- **Francis deSouza takes over as Scale AI CEO tomorrow (Mon, Aug 10).** No new hires or departures surfaced across the data vendors or frontier labs this weekend. The ex-Google Cloud COO's first moves — doubling down on enterprise/government versus re-contesting frontier-lab data work — will set the competitive frame for the quarter. [Scale AI](https://scale.com/blog/scale-appoints-new-ceo), [Axios](https://www.axios.com/2026/07/30/scale-ai-google-cloud-coo-francis-desouza)\n"
