    "markdown": "**Top of mind:** Meta entered the coding-agent race on Aug 5 with Muse Code — and a \"contributor tier\" that sells tokens at ~$0.30/1M in exchange for rights to train on your code. That's a frontier lab building a direct user-data pipeline instead of buying it, the clearest structural challenge yet to bulk data vendors. Meanwhile, Washington confirmed its frontier-model evaluation framework is finished but secret, and synthetic-user startup Simile hit a $2B valuation — money betting that simulated humans can replace some paid human feedback.\n\n## Competitor moves\n\n- **Simile raised $200M at a $2B valuation for \"agentic twin\" simulations of real consumers.** Five months after its $100M Series A, the Joon Sung Park (Stanford \"Smallville\") startup claims 5x revenue growth in 5 months with CVS, Deloitte, and Gallup as clients, simulating audience reactions instead of surveying people. Watch this category: if labs start accepting simulated preference data, the \"verified real human\" value proposition becomes something vendors must actively prove and price. [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/30/synthetic-user-startup-simile-raises-200m-at-2b-valuation-5-months-after-100m-series-a/), [PYMNTS](https://www.pymnts.com/news/artificial-intelligence/2026/simile-raises-200-million-dollars-ai-digital-twin-service/)\n- **Forbes China exposé fallout: quiet so far.** Three days after the report that Surge AI, Mercor, AfterQuery, and Turing supply Chinese labs (~$500M/yr market), no lab or congressional response has surfaced yet — but data-supply-chain attestations in lab procurement remain the thing to prepare for. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/), [AI Weekly](https://aiweekly.co/alerts/surge-ai-mercor-reportedly-sell-training-data-to-chinese-labs)\n\n## New projects & opportunities\n\n- **The US frontier-eval framework is done — and classified, which creates a prep market.** The White House confirmed the voluntary framework from the June EO was completed by its Aug 1 deadline: classified benchmarks, secret thresholds, and a ~30-day pre-release government access window administered by NIST's CAISI; OpenAI, Google, and Anthropic reviewed a draft, and open-weight models are reportedly excluded. Labs must now prep models for tests they can't see — that means demand for proxy evals, adversarial red-teaming, and cyber-capability test sets from outside vendors. [TechPolicy.Press](https://www.techpolicy.press/five-questions-the-us-government-should-answer-about-its-secretive-frontier-ai-framework/), [TNW](https://thenextweb.com/news/white-house-ai-framework-secret-voluntary-classified), [Yahoo News](https://www.yahoo.com/news/politics/articles/white-house-ai-framework-excludes-073920495.html)\n- **EU AI Act labeling and transparency rules are now enforceable against US vendors, per this week's roundups.** Training-data summaries, AI-content labeling, and transparency documentation are now fineable obligations for anyone selling into the EU — data-provenance documentation and compliance-ready dataset audit trails become a billable add-on for vendors serving EU-bound clients. [AI to ROI (Aug 7)](https://ai2roi.substack.com/p/ai-to-roi-news-and-analysis-august)\n\n## Frontier lab movements\n\n- **Meta launched Muse Code (Aug 5), a terminal coding agent on its new Muse Spark 1.2 model — with a data-for-discount tier.** It runs multi-step SWE tasks with sub-agents, and the ~$0.30/1M-token \"contributor tier\" grants Meta training rights over your code. Two implications: a fourth lab is now iterating hard on coding agents (sustained demand for SWE trajectories, code-review data, and agentic evals), and labs harvesting interaction data directly from users squeezes the market for undifferentiated bulk data — expert, verified, rights-clean work is the defensible layer. [CNBC](https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/05/meta-debuts-muse-code-to-take-on-anthropic-and-openai-.html), [TechCrunch](https://techcrunch.com/2026/08/05/meta-launches-muse-code-an-ai-agent-for-large-code-bases/), [Engadget](https://www.engadget.com/2231285/meta-introduces-muse-code-its-take-on-a-coding-agent/)\n- **OpenAI shipped another GPT-5.6 round on Aug 6: Sol improvements plus GPT-5.6 Luna expanded to free users.** The near-weekly update cadence means continuous post-training cycles — steady, recurring demand for preference data and regression evals rather than one-off dataset builds. [OpenAI (PDF)](https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/GPT_5_6_August_Updates.pdf), [LLM Stats](https://llm-stats.com/ai-news)\n\n## Research papers\n\n- **LongHorizon-Harness: move task-state tracking outside the model, verify against the environment (AMAP-ML, Alibaba — Aug 3).** A Manage-Execute-Audit loop with a read-only auditor lifts Terminal-Bench 2.1 from 69.7% to 77.2% and OSWorld 2.0 from 2.8% to 8.3% (Qwen 3.7-Plus), with gains across models. Verification-heavy harness design is exactly the kind of work labs outsource: human-verified environment states, audit rubrics, and failure-mode annotation. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.01964), [HF Papers](https://huggingface.co/papers/2608.01964), [GitHub](https://github.com/AMAP-ML/LongHorizon-Harness)\n- **OSReward: VLM judges of computer-use agents are systematically too lenient — human annotation still wins (HKU NLP / OS-Copilot — Jul 30).** The benchmark finds even frontier judges mislabel failed runs as successes; the team releases OS-Shepherd-100K (human reasoning-annotated trajectory judgments) and open 9B/35B reward models that match commercial judges at 30–60% lower cost. Double-edged for the industry: strong sales evidence for multi-stage human QC on agent-trajectory data now, and a preview of cheap auto-grading later — worth adopting internally before clients use it to push rates down. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28609), [Project page](https://os-copilot.github.io/OSReward-Home/), [HF Papers](https://huggingface.co/papers/2607.28609)\n\n## Leadership movements\n\n- **Calendar note: Francis deSouza formally takes the Scale AI CEO seat on Monday (Aug 10).** No new hires or departures surfaced across the data vendors or frontier labs in the last 48 hours; deSouza's first public moves will show whether Scale keeps ceding frontier-lab labeling ground to Surge and Mercor. [Scale AI](https://scale.com/blog/scale-appoints-new-ceo), [PR Newswire](https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/scale-ai-appoints-francis-desouza-as-ceo-to-lead-next-phase-of-companys-growth-302838437.html)\n"
