    "markdown": "**Top of mind:** Forbes reported Aug 5 that Surge AI, Mercor, Turing, and AfterQuery have been quietly selling expert training data to China's top labs — Tencent, Alibaba, ByteDance, and Ant Group — even as they supply OpenAI, Anthropic, and US federal customers. If frontier labs or Washington respond with data-supply-chain requirements, provably neutral vendors gain a real procurement edge. Closer to home, EXL completed its up-to-$310M acquisition of iMerit on Aug 3 — the clearest signal yet that India-anchored data capability commands strategic premiums.\n\n## Competitor moves\n\n- **Forbes: US data platforms are supplying China's frontier labs.** Documents reviewed by Forbes show Mercor working with Tencent and Alibaba (China ≈2% of Q2 revenue on a ~$2B annualized run rate, with a dedicated China engagement manager), Surge AI supplying Tencent — CEO Edwin Chen traveled to China to meet lab executives — AfterQuery working with Ant Group and Alibaba, and Turing reportedly with ByteDance. Expect labs to start asking every vendor where its data flows; a clean answer becomes a sales asset. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/)\n- **EXL closed its up-to-$310M acquisition of iMerit (Aug 3).** The NASDAQ-listed analytics firm absorbs iMerit's foundation-model annotation, evaluation, and RLHF capability, and founder-CEO Radha Basu joins EXL's executive committee. Consolidation is pricing India-anchored expert-data operations at strategic premiums — and drops a well-capitalized new competitor into enterprise data work. [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/08/03/3337470/9060/en/EXL-completes-acquisition-of-iMerit-accelerating-enterprise-AI-leadership.html), [EXL newsroom](https://www.exlservice.com/about/newsroom/exl-completes-acquisition-of-imerit-accelerating-enterprise-ai-leadership)\n\n## New projects & opportunities\n\n- **Teen-safety evaluation just became a data category.** OpenAI's Aug 6 GPT-5.6 update includes its first dedicated under-18 evaluations measuring model behavior against teen-specific requirements. Every lab will need age-appropriateness eval sets, teen-persona red-teaming, and localized minor-safety data — a new line item worth pitching, including Indic-language coverage. [OpenAI (PDF)](https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/GPT_5_6_August_Updates.pdf)\n- **A \"neutral vendor\" window is opening after the China exposé.** The Forbes story notes the US has restricted chips but not expert-made datasets; if data exports draw scrutiny, US labs will want attestations about who else their vendors serve. India-based suppliers with documented no-China pipelines can turn compliance into differentiation now, before it is table stakes. [Forbes](https://www.forbes.com/sites/annatong/2026/08/05/silicon-valleys-other-china-problem-its-training-their-ai/)\n\n## Frontier lab movements\n\n- **Meta is turning its engineering org into a coding-data factory (Aug 6).** VP Maher Saba asked engineers company-wide to submit at least one code-fix \"diff\" per week through MetaCode; corrections have already improved Muse Spark 1.1 and will post-train an upcoming model known internally as Watermelon. This extends spring's move of ~6,500 engineers into its Agent Data Optimization unit — near-term it means Meta buys less outside coding data, but it also validates senior-engineer corrections as the scarcest input, which external expert networks can supply without the internal-morale cost. [DIGITIMES](https://www.digitimes.com/news/a20260806VL215/meta-ai-agent-coding-training-anthropic-openai.html), [The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/meta-metacode-engineer-training/)\n- **OpenAI refreshed GPT-5.6 Sol and Luna with a new safety report and deeper price cuts (Aug 6).** Both updated models were assessed below Critical under its Preparedness Framework, the U18 evals debuted, and Luna pricing fell 80%. Ever-cheaper frontier inference keeps pushing lab differentiation toward proprietary human data and evaluation rather than raw scale. [OpenAI (PDF)](https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/GPT_5_6_August_Updates.pdf), [Releasebot](https://releasebot.io/updates/openai)\n- **Bloomberg: Google's leadership shuffle complicates its race with OpenAI and Anthropic (Aug 6).** Follow-up to the Aug 5 shakeup covered yesterday — the transition to Koray Kavukcuoglu running DeepMind is being framed as execution risk; vendor and procurement relationships at one of the biggest data buyers are likely to be re-examined. [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2026-08-06/google-s-shakeup-complicates-race-with-openai-anthropic)\n\n## Research papers\n\n- **Echoverse: environment depth — not volume — drives agent-training returns (Microsoft Research, Jul 30).** Yash Pandya, Hussein Mozannar, Corby Rosset, Ahmed Awadallah, Ece Kamar, Akshay Nambi, et al. compile specifications into stateful applications graded against their own databases, with a co-evolution loop that repairs environments from graded rollouts; a 9B model jumps from 36.5% to 67.1%, and four environments ship openly with graders. Bulk environment generation is commoditizing — the paid work shifts to deep, verified environments and grader construction, exactly where human-data vendors can play. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.28074)\n- **Soft inter-rater reliability for free-text annotations (Aug 4).** Pascal Wullschleger, Christian Kreis, Martin A. Walter, Marc Pouly, and Jennifer Foster quantify annotator agreement when labels are open-ended text rather than fixed classes, comparing embedding, LLM-judge, and NLI-based equivalence measures. Practical QA machinery for expert medical and free-text pipelines where classic IRR breaks down — worth folding into delivery QC. [arXiv](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.03529)\n\n## Leadership movements\n\n- **Radha Ramaswami Basu joins EXL as EVP and Head of iMerit** with a seat on the executive committee as the acquisition closed Aug 3. A founder-led integration signals EXL wants iMerit's frontier-lab relationships, not just delivery capacity. [GlobeNewswire](https://www.globenewswire.com/news-release/2026/08/03/3337470/9060/en/EXL-completes-acquisition-of-iMerit-accelerating-enterprise-AI-leadership.html), [EXL newsroom](https://www.exlservice.com/about/newsroom/exl-completes-acquisition-of-imerit-accelerating-enterprise-ai-leadership)\n- **Francis deSouza's last day at Google Cloud is today; he takes over as Scale AI CEO on Monday, Aug 10.** Flagged earlier this week — the handover from interim CEO Jason Droege now begins in earnest, and his first-90-days priorities will show whether Scale re-contests frontier-lab data work. [Axios](https://www.axios.com/2026/07/30/scale-ai-google-cloud-coo-francis-desouza), [Scale AI blog](https://scale.com/blog/scale-appoints-new-ceo)"
